stated general family, not just a checked finite range.\\n\\nSource 1\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n$s$-Inversion Sequences and $P$-Partitions of Type $B$ (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/130942140)\\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.2 years ago; Crawled: 2.4 years ago; Given a sequence $s=(s_1,s_2,\\\\ldots )$ of positive integers, the notion of inversion sequences with respect to $s$, or $s$-inversion sequences, was introduced by Savage and Schuster in their study of lecture hall polytopes. ... Let $I_n$ be the set of $s$-inversion sequences of length $n$ for $s=(1,4,3,8,5,12,\\\\ldots)$---that is, $s_{2i-1}=2i-1$ and $s_{2i}=4i$ for $i\\\\geq1$---and let $P_n$ be the set of signed permutations on the multiset $\\\\{1^2,2^2,\\\\ldots,n^2\\\\}$. ... In this paper, we give a proof of this conjecture by using $P$-partitions of type $B$.\\n\\n# $s$-Inversion Sequences and $P$-Partitions of Type $B$\\n\\nAuthors: William Y. C. Chen, Alan J. X. Guo, Peter L. Guo, Harry H. Y. Huang, and Thomas Y. H. LiuAuthors Info & Affiliations\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/130942140\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nGiven a sequence $s=(s_1,s_2,\\\\ldots )$ of positive integers, the notion of inversion sequences with respect to $s$, or $s$-inversion sequences, was introduced by Savage and Schuster in their study of lecture hall polytopes. A sequence $(e_1,e_2,\\\\ldots,e_n)$ of nonnegative integers is called an $s$-inversion sequence of length $n$ if $0\\\\leq e_i < s_i$ for $1\\\\leq i\\\\leq n$. Let $I_n$ be the set of $s$-inversion sequences of length $n$ for $s=(1,4,3,8,5,12,\\\\ldots)$---that is, $s_{2i-1}=2i-1$ and $s_{2i}=4i$ for $i\\\\geq1$---and let $P_n$ be the set of signed permutations on the multiset $\\\\{1^2,2^2,\\\\ldots,n^2\\\\}$. Savage and Visontai conjectured that the descent number over $P_n$ is equidistributed with the ascent number over $I_{2n}$. In this paper, we give a proof of this conjecture by using $P$-partitions of type $B$. (Lin independently obtained a proof based on recurrence relations.) Moreover, we find a set of signed permutations over which the descent number is equidistributed with the ascent number over $I_{2n-1}$. Let $I'_n$ be the set of $s$-inversion sequences of length $n$ for $s=(2,2,6,4,10,6,\\\\ldots)$; that is, $s_{2i-1}=4i-2$ and $s_{2i}=2i$ for $i\\\\geq1$. We also find two sets of signed permutations over which the descent number is equidistributed with the ascent number over $I'_n$, depending on the parity of $n$.\\n\\n### Keywords\\n\\n  1. inversion sequence\\n  2. ascent number\\n  3. signed permutation\\n  4. descent number\\n  5. $P$-partition of type $B$\\n\\n### MSC codes\\n\\n  1. 05A05\\n  2. 05A15\\n\\n## References\\n\\n1.\\n\\nF. Brenti, $q$-Eulerian polynomials arising from Coxeter groups, European J. Combin., 15 (1994), pp. 417--441.\\n\\nCrossref\\n\\nWeb of Science\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nOn the descent polynomial of signed multipermutations (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258084014_On_the_descent_polynomial_of_signed_multipermutations)\\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Motivated by a conjecture of Savage and Visontai about the equidistribution of the descent statistic on signed permutations of the multiset $\\\\{1,1,2,2,\\\\ldots,n,n\\\\}$ and the ascent statistic on $(1,4,3,8,\\\\ldots,2n-1,4n)$-inversion sequences, we investigate the descent polynomial of the signed permutations of a general multiset. ... For a sequence of positive integers s = {s\\nArticle PDF Available\\n\\n# On the descent polynomial of signed multipermutations\\n\\n  * October 2013\\n  * Proceedings of the American Mathematical Society 143(9)\\n\\nDOI:10.1090/S0002-9939-2015-12555-5\\n\\nAuthors:\\n\\nZhicong Lin\\n\\n  * Claude Bernard University Lyon 1\\n\\nImage\\n\\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nMotivated by a conjecture of Savage and Visontai about the equidistribution of the descent statistic on signed permutations of the multiset $\\\\{1,1,2,2,\\\\ldots,n,n\\\\}$ and the ascent statistic on $(1,4,3,8,\\\\ldots,2n-1,4n)$-inversion sequences, we investigate the descent polynomial of the signed permutations of a general multiset. We obtain a factorial generating function formula for a q-analog of these descent polynomials and apply it to show that they have only real roots. Two different proofs of the conjecture of Savage and Visontai are provided.\\n\\nContent uploaded by Zhicong Lin\\n\\nAuthor content\\n\\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Zhicong Lin on Jun 01, 2015\\n\\nContent may be subject to copyright.\\n\\narXiv:1310.6726v1 [math.CO] 24 Oct 2013\\n\\nON THE DESCENT POLYNOMIAL OF SIGNED\\n\\nMULTIPERMUTATIONS\\n\\nZHICONG LIN\\n\\nAbstract. Motivated by a conjecture of Savage and Visontai about the equidistribution\\n\\nof the descent statistic on signed permutations of the multiset {1, 1, 2, 2, . . ., n, n} and\\n\\nthe ascent statistic on (1, 4, 3, 8, . . . , 2 n − 1, 4 n)-inversion sequences, we investigate the\\n\\ndescent polynomial of the signed permutations of a general multiset. We obtain a factorial\\n\\ngenerating function formula for a q-analog of these descent polynomials and apply it to\\n\\nshow that they have o nly real roots. Two diﬀerent proofs of the conjecture of Savage\\n\\nand Visontai are provided.\\n\\n1. Introduction\\n\\nFor a sequence of positive integers s = {s\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\ns-Inversion Sequences and P-Partitions of Type B (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258082676_s-Inversion_Sequences_and_P-Partitions_of_Type_B)\\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Savage and Visontai [9] proved that for any sequence s of positive integers and any ... Let I n denote the set of s-inversion sequences ... Let P n denote the set of signed permutations on the multiset ... In this paper, we give a proof of Conjecture 1.1.\\n\\nSavage and Visontai [9] proved that for any sequence s of positive integers and any\\n\\npositive integer n, the generating function of the ascent number over s-inversion se-\\n\\nquences of length n has only real roots. Let I n denote the set of s-inversion sequences\\n\\nof length n for the speciﬁc sequence s= (1,4,3,8,5,12,...), that is, for i≥1, s 2 i= 4 i\\n\\nand s 2 i−1= 2 i−1. Let P n denote the set of signed permutations on the multiset\\n\\n{1 2,2 2,...,n 2}. Savage and Visontai [9] posed the following conjecture, which implies\\n\\nthe real-rootedness of the generating function of the descent number over P n.\\n\\nConjecture 1.1 ([9, Conjecture 3.27]) For n≥1, the descent number over P n is equidis-\\n\\ntributed with the ascent number over I 2 n.\\n\\nIn this paper, we give a proof of Conjecture 1.1. Let P n(x) denote the generating\\n\\nfunction of the descent number over the set P n of signed permutations on {1 2,2 2,...,n 2},\\n\\nand let I n(x) denote the generating function of the ascent number over I n. Savage and\\n\\nSchuster [8] found a relation for I n(x). We show that the generating function P n(x)\\n\\nequals the generating function of the descent number over linear extensions of certain\\n\\nsigned labeled forests. By using P-partitions of type B introduced by Chow [5], we show\\n\\nthat the generating function for the descent number over linear extensions satisﬁes the\\n\\nsame relation as I 2 n(x). Thus the generating function P n(x) satisﬁes the same relation\\n\\nas I 2 n(x). This proves Conjecture 1.1.\\n\\nWe also ﬁnd characterizations of signed permutations such that the descent number is\\n\\nequidistributed with the ascent number over three other classes of s-inversion sequences.\\n\\nTo be speciﬁc, we show that the descent number over the set of signed permutations on\\n\\nthe multiset {1 2,2 2,...,(n−1)